# This file's presence is the whole point — GitHub Pages checks that it exists
# and never reads what is in it, so this note lives here rather than in a
# second file that would go stale.
#
# Without it, Pages runs Jekyll over this folder and turns every .md in it into
# a public web page with a theme. That published 52 of them: the release notes
# and the decision log, but also competitor-research, hermes-agent-teardown and
# the whole opencode-study folder — working notes, on the product's own domain,
# indexable, with no way back to the landing page from any of them.
#
# The site is one page. The documents stay in the repository, where anyone who
# wants them can read them on GitHub, and stop being pages nobody designed.
